WEBVTT

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.868
Lieutenant Thomas Eugene Paris,

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:13.276
I hereby reduce you
to the rank of Ensign.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:16.247
And I sentence you to 30
days solitary confinement.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:17.977
Take Ensign Paris to the brig.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.688
I know the way.

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.281
Brig.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.409
One... two... three...

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:47.725
98... 99... 100.

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.091
Staying in shape?

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.527
Oh, yeah.

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:52.189
I do it every time I'm in jail.

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:53.698
Well, I hope

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.358
all that exercise
gave you an appetite.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:56.959
Leola root stew again?

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.300
That's the third time
this week.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:01.932
Can't you just
replicate me a pizza?

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:03.202
Sorry, Tom.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:04.709
Basic nutrition only.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:05.903
Captain's orders.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:08.707
Ah, bread and water, huh?

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.646
Let's have it.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.547
I got you the padd
you asked for.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.485
Oh... thanks.

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:17.489
So...

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:21.584
did you ask the warden
about those holodeck privileges?

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.492
Uh, she said, and I quote:

00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:26.623
"Tell Mr. Paris this is
punishment, not shore leave."

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.062
It was worth a try.

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.067
Well...

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.433
see you next meal.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.869
Wait, leaving already?

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.201
Uh, pull up a chair.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.673
Stay awhile.

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:39.678
Sorry. No nonessential
conversation with the prisoner.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.515
Do the words "cruel and unusual"
mean anything to her?

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.848
I'm telling you, Neelix,

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.386
the inmates are getting
restless.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:51.184
She's going to have a full-blown
prison riot on her hands.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.648
Begin letter.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.279
Dear Father...

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.151
pause... and erase.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.383
Begin.

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:31.919
To Admiral Paris...

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:34.192
pause... and erase.

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:42.840
Begin.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:44.809
Hey, Dad...

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:48.040
Long time, no see.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:51.447
Chances are you'll never
receive this letter...

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:55.647
but in case you do, there's
a few things I wanted to say.

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:00.619
First of all...

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:02.661
bad news.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:07.632
Um... I'm in jail again.

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:10.829
Wait! Keep listening.

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:12.996
Don't turn this off.

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:16.041
I want you to know
how I ended up in here...

00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:19.875
because...

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.210
it's not what you think.

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:26.652
It all started on a morning
I was doing something

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.279
you would find
a complete waste of time.

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:37.862
Captain Proton
to Buster Kincaid.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.593
I've destroyed Dr. Chaotica's
mind control machine

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:44.898
and I'm on my way back
to the spaceship.

00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:46.598
Hello, Proton.

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:49.440
Who is this?

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:51.704
An old friend.

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:54.804
Make that two old friends.

00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.645
The Twin Mistresses of Evil...

00:06:57.717 --> 00:06:59.480
I should have known.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:04.319
I'm afraid your trusty sidekick
is indisposed at the moment.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.187
Don't worry about me, Captain.

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.487
I can handle myself.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.357
We'll see about that.

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:11.329
Bring me the brain probe.

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:12.364
The what?

00:07:12.432 --> 00:07:15.299
The brain probe,
you insolent fool.

00:07:15.368 --> 00:07:17.302
Oh, right.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.600
Your wish is my command.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:21.196
Sorry, Harry.

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:22.901
No problem.

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:25.808
Torture me all you want,
Demonica.

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:26.902
I'll never crack.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.776
Oh, but you will.

00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:30.713
By the time
we're through with you,

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.581
you'll be begging to tell us
everything you know.

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:35.917
You'll be our puppet.

00:07:36.022 --> 00:07:38.490
Our slave.

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.048
Great.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.320
You're doing great.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.621
You're done for, Demonica.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:48.826
Malicia.
She's Demonica.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:50.232
Whatever.

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.670
You two are going to jail
for a very long time.

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.366
- Ooh.
- Ooh.

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:57.204
Move it, toots.

00:07:57.276 --> 00:07:58.641
He's so brave.

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.305
It's a shame
we'll have to kill him.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.871
I've got everything
under control, Proton.

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.816
Shouldn't you be
getting back to headquarters?

00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:08.786
And leave you at the mercy
of these two?

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:11.085
Come on, Tom. I'm just
getting to the good part.

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.489
Senior officers,
report to the Bridge.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:16.153
I guess the good part
will have to wait.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:18.197
Any progress
on those star charts?

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:20.027
I'll have them to you by 1400.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:23.068
1400? You told me they'd
be done yesterday.

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:24.067
I'm sorry, Tom.

00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:25.865
The sensor array went off-line.

00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:28.998
Seven of Nine will have it
up and running this morning.

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:30.167
1400, I promise.

00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:31.267
You need some help?

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:33.141
I'm not on duty again
till tomorrow morning.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:35.476
No, I don't want
to spoil your day off.

00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:37.641
Oh, I don't mind.

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:39.344
Thanks, anyway.

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:40.908
See you later, Lieutenant.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.214
Buster.

00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:45.884
Bye.

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:49.595
I think Jenny really likes you.

00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:51.061
Yeah, I know.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:52.859
I thought
the feeling was mutual.

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:54.900
How many times
do I have to tell you?

00:08:54.967 --> 00:08:57.561
I like Megan, but she won't
give me the time of day.

00:08:57.637 --> 00:08:58.865
What is the difference?

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:00.064
You can't be serious.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:01.731
They're the Delaney sisters,
Harry.

00:09:01.807 --> 00:09:02.865
They're twins.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:03.873
Are you kidding?

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.068
They're nothing alike.

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:07.237
Jenny's aggressive,
and sometimes annoying,

00:09:07.313 --> 00:09:08.871
but Megan...
she's quiet, artistic

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:11.882
and she's got that cute little
dimple in her right cheek.

00:09:12.018 --> 00:09:13.713
Jenny doesn't have the dimple?

00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:15.344
No dimple.

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:17.116
Hmm.

00:09:18.558 --> 00:09:20.583
Bridge.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.185
Well, you've done it
again, Harry.

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:24.490
What?

00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:27.294
Fallen
for the unattainable woman.

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:30.164
First it was a hologram,
then a Borg,

00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:32.170
and now, the wrong twin.

00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:34.069
At least I'm consistent.

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:39.538
Sorry to interrupt
your fun, gentlemen,

00:09:39.612 --> 00:09:40.670
but long-range sensors

00:09:40.746 --> 00:09:42.304
have picked up
something interesting.

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:44.440
A concentrated mass
of oxygen and hydrogen.

00:09:44.517 --> 00:09:46.041
Lots of animal and plant life.

00:09:47.553 --> 00:09:49.043
An M-Class planet?

00:09:49.121 --> 00:09:50.816
No.
That's the interesting part.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.551
We're entering visual range.

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:54.593
On screen.

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:59.554
What is it?

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:01.862
It's an ocean.

00:10:09.075 --> 00:10:10.337
According to these readings,

00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:13.640
it's bigger than the Atlantic
and Pacific combined.

00:10:13.713 --> 00:10:15.271
What's holding it together?

00:10:15.348 --> 00:10:17.316
Looks like there's some
sort of a containment field

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:18.441
keeping it from dissipating.

00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:20.314
Take us in closer, Tom.

00:10:28.394 --> 00:10:30.862
Are those starships
or submarines?

00:10:34.934 --> 00:10:36.333
Open a channel.

00:10:37.837 --> 00:10:40.305
This is Captain Janeway
of the Starship Voyager.

00:10:40.373 --> 00:10:41.806
Please identify yourselves.

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:43.273
They are powering weapons.

00:10:43.342 --> 00:10:44.366
Shields.

00:10:45.811 --> 00:10:46.971
Red Alert.

00:10:47.113 --> 00:10:48.580
Shields holding.
No damage.

00:10:48.648 --> 00:10:49.774
Should I return fire?

00:10:49.849 --> 00:10:50.975
Not yet.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.677
Voyager to approaching vessels.

00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:54.276
We have no hostile intentions.

00:10:55.321 --> 00:10:56.549
They're maintaining
their course.

00:10:56.622 --> 00:10:59.056
Target the lead ship
and take out their weapons.

00:11:05.731 --> 00:11:07.198
Direct hit.

00:11:07.266 --> 00:11:08.631
They're hailing.

00:11:08.701 --> 00:11:10.692
I guess we got their attention.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:11.930
On screen.

00:11:12.038 --> 00:11:13.630
I'm Deputy Consul Burkus

00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:15.640
of the Monean
Maritime Sovereignty.

00:11:15.708 --> 00:11:18.734
You violated our space.

00:11:18.811 --> 00:11:21.075
Withdraw or we'll resume firing.

00:11:21.147 --> 00:11:23.809
Consul, we could have destroyed
your ships, but we didn't.

00:11:23.883 --> 00:11:25.316
We have no interest in a fight.

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:27.045
Then why are you here?

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:28.450
Because we're explorers

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:30.546
and we're fascinated
by your ocean,

00:11:30.623 --> 00:11:32.784
and we'd like to learn
more about it

00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:34.951
and your people,
if you'd be willing.

00:11:35.094 --> 00:11:37.255
And if we are not?

00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:39.991
We'll be disappointed,
but we'll leave you alone.

00:11:42.134 --> 00:11:43.829
Your ship's impressive.

00:11:43.903 --> 00:11:46.394
Well, I'd be happy
to give you a tour.

00:11:48.274 --> 00:11:49.434
Forgive our vigilance, Captain.

00:11:49.508 --> 00:11:51.442
Over the years, my government
has had to protect our ocean

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.603
from more than one
hostile species.

00:11:53.679 --> 00:11:54.805
I understand.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:57.314
We're also very protective
of our own natural resources.

00:11:57.383 --> 00:11:58.372
...about new cultures.

00:11:58.451 --> 00:11:59.782
Yours sounds
particularly fascinating.

00:11:59.852 --> 00:12:01.911
And this is Voyager's
command center.

00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:03.784
Feel free to have a look around.

00:12:03.856 --> 00:12:05.483
I'm curious, Consul.

00:12:05.558 --> 00:12:07.685
Have your people
always lived here?

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.250
Our ancestors were nomadic.

00:12:09.328 --> 00:12:11.922
They only discovered
the waters 300 years ago.

00:12:12.064 --> 00:12:13.929
I'll bet they were
as stunned as we were

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:16.365
to find this huge ball
of water floating in space.

00:12:16.435 --> 00:12:17.595
Yes.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:18.602
Mister?

00:12:18.671 --> 00:12:20.161
Paris. Tom Paris.

00:12:20.239 --> 00:12:22.469
My First Officer,
Commander Chakotay.

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:24.532
They realized they could
farm sea vegetation,

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:25.872
extract oxygen from the ocean

00:12:25.945 --> 00:12:29.676
for their ships...
create a permanent home.

00:12:29.749 --> 00:12:31.114
What's your population?

00:12:31.183 --> 00:12:33.344
More than 80,000.

00:12:33.419 --> 00:12:36.217
And you all live underwater?

00:12:36.288 --> 00:12:39.724
Lieutenant, we do have
other business to attend to.

00:12:39.792 --> 00:12:42.090
Mr. Tuvok, escort our guests
to the briefing room.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.620
Care to join us, Mr. Paris?

00:12:45.698 --> 00:12:47.359
How could you tell?

00:12:51.971 --> 00:12:54.496
Do you still live
aboard your ships?

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:56.700
We've built an industrial
infrastructure

00:12:56.776 --> 00:12:57.970
and undersea dwellings,

00:12:58.043 --> 00:13:00.603
but yes, most of our people
still choose to live

00:13:00.679 --> 00:13:01.805
as our ancestors did.

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:04.076
Any idea how the ocean
came into existence?

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.345
In my experience,
it's a unique phenomenon.

00:13:06.418 --> 00:13:07.680
Riga?

00:13:07.753 --> 00:13:09.721
There are several theories.

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:11.780
Our clerics teach
that the ocean

00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:14.121
was a divine gift
from the creators

00:13:14.193 --> 00:13:15.592
to protect and sustain us,

00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:18.789
but, in my opinion, the most
plausible explanation is

00:13:18.864 --> 00:13:21.856
that the ocean formed naturally,
much the same way

00:13:21.934 --> 00:13:23.265
that a gas giant does.

00:13:23.335 --> 00:13:24.495
Makes sense.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.265
Unfortunately,
our limited knowledge

00:13:26.338 --> 00:13:30.365
of the phenomenon has created
a few problems.

00:13:30.442 --> 00:13:32.034
What do you mean?

00:13:32.111 --> 00:13:34.944
I'm not sure
this is an appropriate topic.

00:13:35.014 --> 00:13:36.709
But they might be able
to help us.

00:13:36.782 --> 00:13:38.545
We'll do anything we can.

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:40.585
The ocean's losing containment.

00:13:40.653 --> 00:13:43.850
Hydro-volume has decreased
more than seven percent

00:13:43.923 --> 00:13:44.912
in the last year alone.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:46.150
Any idea what's causing it?

00:13:46.225 --> 00:13:47.157
No.

00:13:47.226 --> 00:13:48.750
To make a thorough study,

00:13:48.828 --> 00:13:51.296
we'd need to explore
the ocean's center

00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:53.354
where I believe
the gravitational currents

00:13:53.432 --> 00:13:55.127
are fluctuating.

00:13:55.201 --> 00:13:57.135
But that's more
than 600 kilometers deep.

00:13:57.203 --> 00:14:01.367
Our best research vessel
can only go 100 kilometers.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.466
Beyond that,
the pressure's too great.

00:14:04.543 --> 00:14:06.135
Well, we could take you there.

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:08.407
Captain?

00:14:14.086 --> 00:14:16.350
I had no idea you were
such an old salt.

00:14:16.422 --> 00:14:17.889
When I saw that ocean today,

00:14:17.957 --> 00:14:20.926
it reminded me of the first
time I read Jules Verne.

00:14:20.993 --> 00:14:22.585
20,000 Leagues Under The Sea.

00:14:22.661 --> 00:14:23.787
Mmm!

00:14:23.863 --> 00:14:26.491
I must have read it
20,000 times.

00:14:26.565 --> 00:14:28.931
I was obsessed
with stories about the ocean.

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:31.901
All of my friends were busy
with their holo-programs.

00:14:31.971 --> 00:14:34.337
I had my head buried
in Captains Courageous,

00:14:34.406 --> 00:14:35.395
Moby Dick...

00:14:35.474 --> 00:14:36.907
So your interest
in history includes

00:14:36.976 --> 00:14:38.170
the 19th century, as well.

00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:41.839
Ancient sailing ships
were always my first love.

00:14:41.914 --> 00:14:43.472
I had it all planned.

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:47.679
Finish high school, join
the Federation Naval Patrol...

00:14:47.753 --> 00:14:51.052
but my father had other ideas.

00:14:51.123 --> 00:14:52.613
You'd think Admiral Paris

00:14:52.691 --> 00:14:54.716
might have understood
his son's passion.

00:14:54.793 --> 00:14:57.125
Hmm...

00:14:57.196 --> 00:14:58.959
As far as he was concerned,

00:14:59.098 --> 00:15:00.929
the only ship
I was going to serve on

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:02.900
had to have
a Starfleet insignia on it.

00:15:03.035 --> 00:15:05.469
So now you have an opportunity
to make up for lost time.

00:15:06.672 --> 00:15:08.196
Captain...

00:15:08.274 --> 00:15:10.174
with a few simple
thruster modifications

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:12.802
to the Delta Flyer, she will
be seaworthy in no time.

00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:13.810
Good.

00:15:13.879 --> 00:15:15.278
It'd take at least a week

00:15:15.347 --> 00:15:17.907
to make the necessary
modifications to Voyager.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.779
Then it's my mission?

00:15:19.852 --> 00:15:21.945
Bon voyage.

00:15:27.793 --> 00:15:29.556
And so, I thought to myself,

00:15:29.628 --> 00:15:32.426
who better than Harry
to be my first mate?

00:15:32.498 --> 00:15:33.430
First mate?

00:15:33.499 --> 00:15:34.431
Oh... sailor talk.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:35.831
You'll get the hang of it.

00:15:35.901 --> 00:15:36.833
Ah.

00:15:36.902 --> 00:15:38.062
I'm telling you, Harry,

00:15:38.137 --> 00:15:40.128
I have been dreaming
about something like this

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:41.467
for as long as I can remember.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:42.666
Correct me if I'm wrong,

00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:44.732
but there is a mission
involved here, right?

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:45.742
Of course!

00:15:45.811 --> 00:15:46.937
But there's no law that says

00:15:47.079 --> 00:15:49.138
we can't have a little
fun along the way.

00:15:49.214 --> 00:15:50.613
Ah, bosun.

00:15:50.683 --> 00:15:52.651
Ready to shove off?

00:15:52.718 --> 00:15:54.618
What are you talking
about, Lieutenant?

00:15:54.687 --> 00:15:55.915
Call me Skipper.

00:15:55.988 --> 00:15:57.785
Sailor talk.
You'll get used to it.

00:15:57.856 --> 00:15:59.084
I think not.

00:15:59.158 --> 00:16:00.955
Hull and thruster reinforcements
are complete.

00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:02.458
Ah, that's
what I wanted to hear.

00:16:02.528 --> 00:16:03.460
Oh, excuse me.

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:05.121
Am I in the right place?

00:16:05.197 --> 00:16:07.495
Welcome aboard.

00:16:07.566 --> 00:16:09.227
I see you like to travel light.

00:16:09.301 --> 00:16:10.700
These are my instruments

00:16:10.769 --> 00:16:12.999
for measuring depth,
pressure, currents...

00:16:13.072 --> 00:16:14.164
We will not need them.

00:16:14.239 --> 00:16:16.298
This vessel is equipped
with a complete sensor array.

00:16:16.375 --> 00:16:18.343
- Oh.
- Uh, it's okay.

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:21.072
You can store your gear
in the hold.

00:16:36.295 --> 00:16:38.559
20,000 kilometers
to the surface.

00:16:39.398 --> 00:16:40.922
Immersion shielding?

00:16:41.066 --> 00:16:42.397
Active.

00:16:44.503 --> 00:16:46.061
Anchors aweigh.

00:17:13.132 --> 00:17:15.396
Those structures, what are they?

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:19.403
It's our main oxygen refinery
and desalination plant.

00:17:19.471 --> 00:17:23.635
Corrosion resistant alloys,
variable-density ballast...

00:17:23.709 --> 00:17:25.233
an efficient design.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.244
That means she's impressed.

00:17:27.312 --> 00:17:30.748
We're very proud
of what we've built here.

00:17:30.816 --> 00:17:32.113
I can see why.

00:17:39.224 --> 00:17:40.282
Come in.

00:17:44.229 --> 00:17:46.288
I'm afraid we have
some disturbing news.

00:17:46.365 --> 00:17:48.333
Oh?

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:49.924
We've run a computer simulation

00:17:50.002 --> 00:17:52.129
to determine
the rate of dissipation.

00:17:52.204 --> 00:17:54.138
It's worse
than Mr. Riga thought.

00:17:54.206 --> 00:17:55.537
According to our estimates,

00:17:55.607 --> 00:17:58.804
the ocean could experience
a complete loss of containment

00:17:58.877 --> 00:18:00.208
in less than five years.

00:18:00.279 --> 00:18:02.372
I'm sorry.

00:18:02.448 --> 00:18:04.382
Your calculations
could be wrong.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:05.974
I know it sounds grim,

00:18:06.051 --> 00:18:08.815
but there may be some
way to stop the process.

00:18:08.887 --> 00:18:10.616
And if there isn't?

00:18:12.958 --> 00:18:15.324
You might have to
consider evacuating.

00:18:15.394 --> 00:18:18.454
I'm supposed to go back
and explain this

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.498
to 47 regional sovereigns?

00:18:20.566 --> 00:18:23.899
They'll pass their first
unanimous resolution...

00:18:23.969 --> 00:18:25.493
calling for my head.

00:18:25.571 --> 00:18:26.833
I can imagine

00:18:26.905 --> 00:18:28.896
how difficult this must be
for you...

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:31.738
but you will have
to tell them.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:33.243
Perhaps...

00:18:33.312 --> 00:18:37.271
but I'll wait until
your Delta Flyer returns.

00:18:37.349 --> 00:18:40.716
Maybe they'll find
something tangible.

00:18:43.155 --> 00:18:46.124
We're at a depth
of 560 kilometers.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.262
What was that?

00:18:56.335 --> 00:18:57.859
The hull contracting.

00:18:57.936 --> 00:19:01.736
Rerouting additional power
to structural integrity.

00:19:01.807 --> 00:19:03.536
Nothing to worry about.

00:19:03.609 --> 00:19:10.139
I'm detecting multiphasic energy
discharges, bearing 021 mark 6.

00:19:10.215 --> 00:19:12.376
Range: 12 kilometers.

00:19:12.451 --> 00:19:13.782
A structure.

00:19:13.852 --> 00:19:15.114
At this depth?

00:19:15.187 --> 00:19:16.814
Adjusting course.

00:19:19.958 --> 00:19:21.118
I can't see anything.

00:19:21.193 --> 00:19:24.390
Give me forward illumination,
Harry.

00:19:27.766 --> 00:19:29.233
What is it?

00:19:29.301 --> 00:19:32.395
It's generating massive amounts
of artificial gravity.

00:19:32.471 --> 00:19:35.201
Looks like
some kind of field reactor.

00:19:35.274 --> 00:19:36.206
If it's malfunctioning,

00:19:36.275 --> 00:19:38.072
that would explain
the loss of hydro-volume.

00:19:38.143 --> 00:19:40.703
Maybe it can be repaired.

00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:42.576
It looks ancient.

00:19:48.654 --> 00:19:50.622
Well, if these readings
are right,

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:52.714
it's at least 100,000 years old.

00:19:54.493 --> 00:19:57.155
We've no records
of any previous inhabitants.

00:19:57.229 --> 00:19:58.696
Who built it?
Where did they go?

00:19:58.764 --> 00:20:00.026
Looks like the reactor

00:20:00.098 --> 00:20:01.622
is controlled
by a core computer.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.431
I'll try to upload the database
and get some answers.

00:20:05.504 --> 00:20:06.801
Initiate the interface.

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:08.464
Upload in progress.

00:20:12.678 --> 00:20:13.770
What's happening?

00:20:13.845 --> 00:20:15.779
We've got a visitor.

00:20:17.516 --> 00:20:19.313
What was it?!

00:20:19.384 --> 00:20:20.908
You're the one that lives here.
You tell us.

00:20:20.986 --> 00:20:23.477
No one's ever been
this far down before.

00:20:23.555 --> 00:20:26.080
We don't know anything
about marine life

00:20:26.158 --> 00:20:27.182
at these depths.

00:20:27.259 --> 00:20:29.250
You are about
to have the opportunity

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:30.818
to make a detailed study.

00:20:40.038 --> 00:20:42.199
The creature's emitting
biothermic discharges.

00:20:42.274 --> 00:20:45.141
It's like some kind
of electric eel.

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:47.508
Only a hell of a lot bigger.

00:20:50.382 --> 00:20:52.373
And significantly more powerful.

00:20:52.451 --> 00:20:55.147
That last discharge
exceeded 500,000 volts.

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:56.482
The shields are fried, Tom.

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:59.854
You might want to think
about getting us out of here.

00:21:05.163 --> 00:21:07.393
Thrusters are off-line.

00:21:07.466 --> 00:21:09.457
Targeting forward phasers.

00:21:09.534 --> 00:21:10.558
No, you can't kill it.

00:21:10.636 --> 00:21:13.196
I do not intend to.

00:21:15.874 --> 00:21:18.342
Unfortunately, the creature
does not seem to be

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:19.536
of a similar opinion.

00:21:19.611 --> 00:21:20.771
How's that upload coming?

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:22.040
Another couple of minutes.

00:21:22.114 --> 00:21:23.638
Firing phasers.

00:21:27.552 --> 00:21:29.884
I think we only made him madder.

00:21:31.023 --> 00:21:32.513
The creature is retreating.

00:21:37.029 --> 00:21:38.155
We've got a breach!

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.164
I'm on it.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:48.298
600 kilometers underwater,
propulsion off-line,

00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:49.738
water pouring into the cabin...

00:21:50.976 --> 00:21:51.908
It was like something

00:21:51.977 --> 00:21:53.501
out of one
of those Jules Verne stories

00:21:53.578 --> 00:21:54.977
you used to read me
when I was a kid.

00:21:55.113 --> 00:21:56.978
Red Alert!

00:21:57.082 --> 00:21:59.243
All hands to battle stations.

00:21:59.318 --> 00:22:00.307
Hey, what about me?

00:22:00.385 --> 00:22:01.409
If we're in trouble,

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:03.386
we need our best pilot
at the helm.

00:22:03.455 --> 00:22:05.082
You can't just leave me here!

00:22:19.838 --> 00:22:20.930
Five ships?

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:23.564
And they just opened
fire without warning?

00:22:23.642 --> 00:22:24.768
- Mm-hmm.
- Oh...

00:22:24.843 --> 00:22:25.935
How did we get away?

00:22:26.044 --> 00:22:28.604
Apparently, Ensign Culhane
confused the enemy

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:31.240
with a brilliant series
of evasive maneuvers.

00:22:31.316 --> 00:22:33.648
I hear Captain Janeway
is considering him

00:22:33.719 --> 00:22:35.050
for Chief Conn Officer.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.052
Oh, yeah?

00:22:36.121 --> 00:22:37.952
Well, you just tell the Captain

00:22:38.056 --> 00:22:40.115
that Culhane's
brilliant maneuvers

00:22:40.192 --> 00:22:41.955
almost knocked me unconscious.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.392
You'd think he'd never
flown a shuttle,

00:22:44.463 --> 00:22:45.760
much less a starship.

00:22:45.831 --> 00:22:47.128
There we are.

00:22:47.199 --> 00:22:48.461
That's it?

00:22:48.533 --> 00:22:50.967
Aren't you going to run
a neurological scan?

00:22:51.103 --> 00:22:53.628
Maybe I should be granted
a medical reprieve.

00:22:53.705 --> 00:22:57.106
Your injury was what Naomi
Wildman refers to as a boo-boo.

00:22:57.175 --> 00:22:59.268
Come on, Doc. You don't
understand what it's like

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:01.107
being down here
all day, every day.

00:23:01.179 --> 00:23:02.111
I'm going crazy.

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:03.238
Correct me if I'm wrong,

00:23:03.315 --> 00:23:04.373
but isn't that the idea?

00:23:04.449 --> 00:23:05.381
No, seriously, Doc,

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:06.747
you might want to take me down

00:23:06.818 --> 00:23:08.877
to Sick Bay for a full
psychiatric evaluation.

00:23:08.954 --> 00:23:11.286
Three or four days
of observation at least.

00:23:11.356 --> 00:23:13.051
See you in 20 days.

00:23:13.125 --> 00:23:14.786
Doc!

00:23:24.202 --> 00:23:26.636
Resume recording.

00:23:28.707 --> 00:23:31.335
Okay, Dad, where were we?

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:32.672
Ah, right.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:35.235
We had just sprung a leak.

00:23:44.823 --> 00:23:47.690
Nothing like a cold shower
to wake up the senses.

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:49.727
Structural integrity
is weakening.

00:23:49.795 --> 00:23:52.559
We've lost communications,
shields, propulsion.

00:23:52.631 --> 00:23:54.963
We can reduce our density
by venting plasma

00:23:55.033 --> 00:23:57.365
and transporting
all nonessential equipment

00:23:57.436 --> 00:23:58.960
off the ship.

00:23:59.104 --> 00:24:02.267
It will take time, but we will
eventually rise to the surface.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.604
I think we should stay.

00:24:04.676 --> 00:24:06.075
Stay?

00:24:06.144 --> 00:24:09.375
I'm not about to be scared off
by a few damaged systems.

00:24:09.448 --> 00:24:11.780
Tom, don't you think
maybe you're carrying

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:14.341
this Captains Courageous thing
a little too far?

00:24:14.419 --> 00:24:15.681
Look...

00:24:15.754 --> 00:24:17.881
it's a good bet that the
reactor's malfunctioning.

00:24:17.956 --> 00:24:20.015
Now, we're only going to get
one shot at fixing it.

00:24:20.091 --> 00:24:21.183
You want to leave, fine.

00:24:21.259 --> 00:24:22.658
Give me an environmental suit

00:24:22.727 --> 00:24:25.025
and you can pick me up
after you've repaired the Flyer.

00:24:25.096 --> 00:24:27.155
You're going for a swim?
Are you crazy?

00:24:27.232 --> 00:24:29.166
You have a better idea?

00:24:30.535 --> 00:24:32.435
Well...

00:24:32.504 --> 00:24:35.701
we already managed to interface
with the reactor's computer.

00:24:35.774 --> 00:24:38.174
Maybe we can make the repairs,
maybe we can't,

00:24:38.243 --> 00:24:40.404
but I'm not leaving you
down here alone.

00:24:41.913 --> 00:24:43.710
What about you two?

00:24:43.782 --> 00:24:45.647
I'll stay.

00:24:46.651 --> 00:24:47.845
Seven?

00:24:47.919 --> 00:24:50.888
It is in my nature
to comply with the collective.

00:24:59.664 --> 00:25:02.224
I'm detecting a breach
in the containment field.

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:03.494
Any way we can seal it?

00:25:03.568 --> 00:25:05.365
We might try reinforcing
the field

00:25:05.437 --> 00:25:06.699
with a deflector beam.

00:25:06.771 --> 00:25:07.863
Do it.

00:25:07.939 --> 00:25:09.600
Try hailing the
Delta Flyer again.

00:25:12.244 --> 00:25:13.506
Nothing.

00:25:13.578 --> 00:25:15.205
What do you think's happened?

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.044
They may be too deep
to receive a transmission.

00:25:18.116 --> 00:25:19.845
They may have had an accident.

00:25:19.918 --> 00:25:21.249
- It's possible.
- Possible?

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:24.186
Captain, I'll need a better
explanation than that.

00:25:24.256 --> 00:25:26.520
What am I supposed
to tell the Council?

00:25:26.591 --> 00:25:28.024
Clarify something for me.

00:25:28.093 --> 00:25:29.355
Are you more concerned

00:25:29.427 --> 00:25:31.918
about the lives
of the people on that shuttle

00:25:32.063 --> 00:25:33.553
or your political career?

00:25:35.867 --> 00:25:37.459
What's that?

00:25:37.536 --> 00:25:38.935
A gravimetric discharge.

00:25:39.037 --> 00:25:41.062
The reactor's core is unstable.

00:25:41.139 --> 00:25:43.300
You'd be unstable, too, if you
were as old as this thing.

00:25:43.375 --> 00:25:44.808
Age has nothing to do with it.

00:25:44.876 --> 00:25:47.174
The reactor's diverting
massive amounts of power

00:25:47.245 --> 00:25:48.837
to its structural
integrity field,

00:25:48.914 --> 00:25:51.246
power normally reserved
for oceanic containment.

00:25:51.316 --> 00:25:53.375
That would explain why
containment is weakening.

00:25:53.451 --> 00:25:56.614
Looks like the density
of the water's been increasing

00:25:56.688 --> 00:25:57.620
over the past few years.

00:25:57.689 --> 00:25:58.883
It seems the reactor's
just trying

00:25:58.957 --> 00:26:00.219
to keep itself
from being crushed.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:05.796
Can we initiate a power
transfer, stabilize the core?

00:26:05.864 --> 00:26:08.731
I believe so, but it would be
a temporary solution.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:11.667
It's better than nothing.

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:21.807
Captain, full containment
has just been re-established.

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:23.006
The deflector beam.

00:26:23.081 --> 00:26:24.548
Negative. The ocean appears

00:26:24.616 --> 00:26:26.584
to have been stabilized
from within.

00:26:26.651 --> 00:26:29.142
It looks like our team
has succeeded.

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.621
I'm picking up an object
on a slow ascent.

00:26:32.691 --> 00:26:34.488
It's the Flyer.

00:26:37.529 --> 00:26:39.121
You found something?

00:26:39.197 --> 00:26:41.927
I've been studying
the generator's database.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.934
You're not going
to believe this,

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:47.664
but apparently, your ocean
used to be part of a landmass.

00:26:47.739 --> 00:26:49.229
Really?

00:26:49.307 --> 00:26:50.467
As far as I can tell,

00:26:50.542 --> 00:26:52.703
it was part
of a planetary ecosystem

00:26:52.777 --> 00:26:55.268
inhabited by
a very advanced civilization.

00:26:55.347 --> 00:26:56.609
What happened to them?

00:26:56.681 --> 00:26:58.046
That's a good question.

00:26:58.116 --> 00:27:01.643
All I know is they launched
this reactor into orbit.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:03.153
They used some kind

00:27:03.221 --> 00:27:05.212
of elaborate
kinetic transfer system

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:09.124
to draw the water and everything
in it up to the reactor.

00:27:10.862 --> 00:27:13.387
Extraordinary.

00:27:13.465 --> 00:27:16.832
Why would anyone want
to move an entire ocean?

00:27:16.901 --> 00:27:20.200
Maybe there was some kind
of disaster on the planet,

00:27:20.271 --> 00:27:22.535
or maybe it was
just an experiment.

00:27:22.607 --> 00:27:25.303
That must have been
a massive undertaking.

00:27:25.377 --> 00:27:27.174
Took them almost 200 years.

00:27:27.245 --> 00:27:30.476
I wonder what they'd think
if they knew we settled here

00:27:30.548 --> 00:27:32.379
and built another civilization.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:35.908
Actually, I think
they'd be pretty concerned.

00:27:35.987 --> 00:27:37.477
Oh?

00:27:37.555 --> 00:27:40.922
This field reactor
that they designed...

00:27:40.992 --> 00:27:44.928
it's a pretty amazing piece
of technology... durable...

00:27:45.063 --> 00:27:48.931
I don't think it's responsible
for the loss of containment.

00:27:49.067 --> 00:27:52.059
But as soon as we made
the recalibration,

00:27:52.137 --> 00:27:53.729
the water stabilized.

00:27:53.805 --> 00:27:56.330
I don't see
what else it could be.

00:27:56.408 --> 00:28:00.572
Riga, your mining operations
are destroying the ocean.

00:28:07.752 --> 00:28:10.186
You'll never get
into Starfleet Academy

00:28:10.255 --> 00:28:11.586
by playing with toys.

00:28:11.656 --> 00:28:12.714
Do your homework.

00:28:12.791 --> 00:28:14.850
Are you listening to me, Thomas?

00:28:14.926 --> 00:28:16.689
Thomas Eugene Paris,

00:28:16.761 --> 00:28:18.661
I hereby reduce you
to the rank of Ensign.

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:21.198
And I sentence you to 30 years
in solitary confinement.

00:28:21.266 --> 00:28:22.858
Go to your room, young man.

00:28:22.934 --> 00:28:24.026
That's an order.

00:28:24.102 --> 00:28:26.366
Stay there and think
about what I've said.

00:28:26.438 --> 00:28:29.771
Tom... Tom...

00:28:29.841 --> 00:28:31.035
Tom, wake up.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:33.872
Bad dream?

00:28:33.945 --> 00:28:36.072
Oh...

00:28:36.147 --> 00:28:37.637
You could say that.

00:28:42.754 --> 00:28:45.154
You know, you look like hell.

00:28:45.223 --> 00:28:46.884
Thanks.

00:28:46.958 --> 00:28:48.926
How'd you get past the guard?

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:50.925
The Vulcan neck pinch.

00:28:51.029 --> 00:28:52.587
Come to spring me?

00:28:52.664 --> 00:28:55.690
Well, the Captain finally
gave me permission to visit,

00:28:55.767 --> 00:28:57.598
but I've only got a few minutes.

00:28:57.669 --> 00:28:59.296
That's big of her.

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:00.894
How are you doing?

00:29:00.972 --> 00:29:04.430
Oh, 30 days?
No problem.

00:29:04.509 --> 00:29:07.706
How's B'Elanna?

00:29:07.779 --> 00:29:09.576
She misses you.

00:29:09.647 --> 00:29:12.480
Yeah. I know the feeling.

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:15.883
So, what have you been doing
to pass the time?

00:29:15.954 --> 00:29:20.414
Thinking and...

00:29:20.492 --> 00:29:22.858
thinking.

00:29:22.927 --> 00:29:25.521
Trying not to think.

00:29:25.597 --> 00:29:29.624
Actually, I started recording
a letter to my father.

00:29:29.701 --> 00:29:31.293
Really?

00:29:31.369 --> 00:29:33.530
Yeah.

00:29:33.605 --> 00:29:36.005
For some reason, I...

00:29:36.074 --> 00:29:39.601
I wanted to explain to him
how I ended up in here.

00:29:39.677 --> 00:29:41.702
I didn't want him to think...

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:46.308
Well, anyway, I realized
how ridiculous it was.

00:29:46.384 --> 00:29:47.715
You should finish it.

00:29:47.786 --> 00:29:49.253
Give me one good reason.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.151
Maybe he'll hear it someday.

00:29:51.222 --> 00:29:52.416
Doubtful.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.924
You'll feel better
getting it off your chest.

00:29:55.026 --> 00:29:58.052
Are you bucking for
ship's counselor, Harry?

00:29:58.129 --> 00:30:01.030
What are you going to do,
delete the letter?

00:30:01.099 --> 00:30:02.691
That would be pointless.

00:30:02.767 --> 00:30:04.462
At least I'm consistent.

00:30:04.536 --> 00:30:05.468
Tom...

00:30:05.537 --> 00:30:07.505
Thanks for the visit, Harry.

00:30:13.978 --> 00:30:15.570
See you in 14.

00:30:21.119 --> 00:30:23.644
What was it you once told me?

00:30:23.721 --> 00:30:26.713
That your father used to say
you never finished anything?

00:30:42.974 --> 00:30:44.703
Resume recording.

00:30:44.776 --> 00:30:48.974
So, we told Consul Burkus
about the alien field reactor

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.641
and how his oxygen refineries
were destroying it.

00:30:52.717 --> 00:30:56.153
He took the news pretty well...
at first.

00:30:56.221 --> 00:30:59.088
The Council's very grateful
for your help, Captain.

00:30:59.157 --> 00:31:00.624
They've asked me to request

00:31:00.692 --> 00:31:02.592
the shield
and thruster schematics

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.025
for your Delta Flyer.

00:31:04.095 --> 00:31:07.223
We're hoping to design a probe
that'll allow us to monitor

00:31:07.298 --> 00:31:08.697
the containment generator.

00:31:08.766 --> 00:31:09.733
Lieutenant Torres

00:31:09.801 --> 00:31:11.564
will give you
everything you need.

00:31:11.636 --> 00:31:13.365
I've also drawn up some designs

00:31:13.438 --> 00:31:15.235
for an oxygen
replication system.

00:31:15.306 --> 00:31:17.069
It'll allow you
to create free oxygen

00:31:17.141 --> 00:31:18.938
without extricating
it from the water.

00:31:19.077 --> 00:31:21.944
It won't solve your problems
overnight,

00:31:22.046 --> 00:31:23.343
but it's a start.

00:31:23.414 --> 00:31:25.211
I'm sure it'll be very helpful.

00:31:25.283 --> 00:31:28.684
Our oxygen extraction levels
are still dangerously high.

00:31:28.753 --> 00:31:31.881
I'm going to recommend
shutting down refineries

00:31:31.956 --> 00:31:33.287
four, five and six.

00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:36.191
We'll take it
under advisement. Well...

00:31:36.261 --> 00:31:38.422
Captain, I wish you
a safe journey.

00:31:38.496 --> 00:31:40.726
Well, we have
a few more suggestions,

00:31:40.798 --> 00:31:41.958
if you'd like to hear them.

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:44.432
Please... pass them along
to Mr. Riga.

00:31:44.502 --> 00:31:46.367
He'll include them
in his report.

00:31:46.437 --> 00:31:47.461
I'm curious.

00:31:47.538 --> 00:31:49.802
Who's going to read that report?

00:31:49.874 --> 00:31:51.899
It will be given
to the subcommittees

00:31:51.976 --> 00:31:53.705
on life support and agriculture.

00:31:53.778 --> 00:31:55.109
Excuse me, Consul,

00:31:55.179 --> 00:31:58.342
I'm not sure you understand
the magnitude of the crisis.

00:31:58.416 --> 00:32:00.714
What you're suggesting
could take months.

00:32:00.785 --> 00:32:02.810
Thank you, Mr. Riga.

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:04.514
You should listen to him.

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:08.218
If you don't make some serious
changes around here soon,

00:32:08.293 --> 00:32:10.921
that ocean won't be here
much longer.

00:32:10.995 --> 00:32:13.463
As I said,
we understand his concerns.

00:32:13.531 --> 00:32:14.691
Do you? It seems to me

00:32:14.766 --> 00:32:17.234
like you're just trying
to sidestep the issue

00:32:17.302 --> 00:32:18.564
and send us on our way.

00:32:18.636 --> 00:32:19.933
With all due respect,

00:32:20.038 --> 00:32:22.233
who are you
to tell us what to do

00:32:22.307 --> 00:32:23.569
with our ocean?

00:32:23.641 --> 00:32:25.939
With all due respect,
it's not your ocean.

00:32:26.077 --> 00:32:27.840
Lieutenant.

00:32:27.912 --> 00:32:29.436
It's all right, Captain.

00:32:29.514 --> 00:32:31.948
I'd like to respond,
but not as a diplomat

00:32:32.083 --> 00:32:33.175
as a Monean.

00:32:33.251 --> 00:32:35.583
You came here claiming
you wanted to learn

00:32:35.653 --> 00:32:36.813
about our way of life,

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:38.913
and now, having spent
three days here,

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:40.753
you're suggesting we abandon it.

00:32:40.825 --> 00:32:44.158
We have an expression...
brine in the veins.

00:32:44.228 --> 00:32:45.855
Tell him what it means.

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:48.057
It's used to describe someone

00:32:48.132 --> 00:32:51.158
who has a special connection
to the waters.

00:32:51.235 --> 00:32:56.104
My family has lived here
for ten generations.

00:32:56.174 --> 00:33:00.508
We protected this ocean,
cultivated it,

00:33:00.578 --> 00:33:03.342
lived in harmony with
the animals that inhabit it.

00:33:03.414 --> 00:33:05.541
Can you say the same?

00:33:07.418 --> 00:33:08.817
I didn't think so.

00:33:08.886 --> 00:33:11.218
Good day, Captain.

00:33:17.795 --> 00:33:19.387
We can't just let this go.

00:33:19.464 --> 00:33:21.295
What do you want me to do?

00:33:21.366 --> 00:33:24.665
Of course.
The almighty Prime Directive.

00:33:24.736 --> 00:33:27.796
Would you please excuse us,
Lieutenant.

00:33:30.742 --> 00:33:31.902
See you later.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:36.879
I know you're upset, Lieutenant,

00:33:36.948 --> 00:33:38.438
but when you're
in a room with me,

00:33:38.516 --> 00:33:40.746
you check that attitude
at the door. Understood?

00:33:40.818 --> 00:33:41.876
Captain...

00:33:41.953 --> 00:33:43.750
We can't expect
an entire society

00:33:43.821 --> 00:33:46.585
to change because we
think they should.

00:33:46.657 --> 00:33:48.682
- Then you agree with me.
- Yes.

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:50.751
And we gave them
the help they asked for.

00:33:50.828 --> 00:33:52.090
We told them what we know.

00:33:52.163 --> 00:33:54.961
Now it's up to them to do
what they think is appropriate.

00:33:55.099 --> 00:33:55.963
You heard that consul.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:57.226
They're not going to do
a damn thing.

00:33:57.301 --> 00:33:58.529
Maybe not, but that's
their prerogative.

00:33:58.603 --> 00:34:00.901
- Captain...
- End of discussion, Lieutenant.

00:34:00.972 --> 00:34:03.133
At 1400 hours,
we'll resume a course

00:34:03.207 --> 00:34:05.539
for the Alpha Quadrant.
Is that clear?

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:08.670
Is that clear?

00:34:08.746 --> 00:34:10.680
As a bell.

00:34:33.304 --> 00:34:35.135
Hey.

00:34:35.206 --> 00:34:37.766
Here to stamp out
intergalactic evil?

00:34:41.779 --> 00:34:43.246
It's funny.

00:34:45.183 --> 00:34:46.480
What?

00:34:46.551 --> 00:34:48.519
I went on this mission

00:34:48.586 --> 00:34:52.317
expecting to play out
a childhood fantasy,

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:55.553
but along the way,

00:34:55.626 --> 00:35:02.998
when I realized that ocean
would just be... gone one day...

00:35:03.067 --> 00:35:05.433
it started to matter to me.

00:35:09.173 --> 00:35:10.902
Sounds pretty stupid, huh?

00:35:10.975 --> 00:35:12.272
No.

00:35:15.179 --> 00:35:18.444
No, it sounds like you've
found yourself a cause.

00:35:20.785 --> 00:35:23.913
I never thought of myself
as a cause kind of guy.

00:35:24.055 --> 00:35:27.547
Well, for what it's worth,
I'm proud of you.

00:35:29.193 --> 00:35:30.922
Thanks.

00:35:32.964 --> 00:35:36.456
But Captain Proton's
not going to be able

00:35:36.534 --> 00:35:39.367
to save the day this time,
is he?

00:35:39.437 --> 00:35:42.065
What about Tom Paris?

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:52.174
Council Chamber, please.

00:35:52.250 --> 00:35:54.309
Riga...

00:35:54.385 --> 00:35:56.615
You're dismissed, crewman.

00:36:02.727 --> 00:36:03.819
I want you to tell me honestly...

00:36:03.895 --> 00:36:05.362
what do you think's
going to happen now?

00:36:05.429 --> 00:36:06.623
I think the bureaucracy

00:36:06.697 --> 00:36:09.029
isn't going to make
more than token changes.

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:10.692
Isn't there anything else
you can do?

00:36:10.768 --> 00:36:11.894
I don't see how.

00:36:11.969 --> 00:36:15.632
Other than taking the oxygen
refineries off-line.

00:36:17.074 --> 00:36:20.339
What would happen...

00:36:20.411 --> 00:36:22.504
hypothetically...

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:26.880
if someone were to shut down
the refineries?

00:36:26.951 --> 00:36:30.409
Well, I suppose
they'd have to be rebuilt.

00:36:30.488 --> 00:36:32.683
Yeah, and they'd probably
be more willing

00:36:32.757 --> 00:36:34.725
to redesign them
while they're at it.

00:36:34.792 --> 00:36:37.386
If nothing else,
it would grab their attention.

00:36:38.563 --> 00:36:40.053
Are you suggesting?

00:36:40.131 --> 00:36:42.122
I'm just asking questions...

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:45.226
because I'm not supposed
to get involved

00:36:45.303 --> 00:36:48.363
in the internal affairs
of alien worlds...

00:36:48.439 --> 00:36:52.773
unless, of course,
someone makes a direct request.

00:36:57.615 --> 00:37:01.881
If you can get me down there,
I'd be willing to do it.

00:37:01.953 --> 00:37:05.286
You'd be risking your life.

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:07.889
I know.

00:37:11.362 --> 00:37:12.886
Captain, there has just been

00:37:12.997 --> 00:37:15.625
an unauthorized launch
from Shuttle Bay 1...

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:16.894
the Delta Flyer.

00:37:16.968 --> 00:37:19.300
Tom.

00:37:30.381 --> 00:37:31.348
Hail him.

00:37:32.383 --> 00:37:33.372
No response.

00:37:33.451 --> 00:37:34.509
Try a tractor beam.

00:37:34.585 --> 00:37:35.779
We're out of range.

00:37:35.853 --> 00:37:38.117
We're being hailed, Captain.
It's Consul Burkus.

00:37:38.189 --> 00:37:41.090
Your shuttlecraft
has violated our borders.

00:37:41.158 --> 00:37:42.819
I demand an explanation.

00:37:42.893 --> 00:37:45.384
Mr. Paris is acting
without authorization.

00:37:45.463 --> 00:37:46.395
To what end?

00:37:46.464 --> 00:37:49.922
According to our scans,
Mr. Riga is with him.

00:37:50.067 --> 00:37:53.093
I have to assume they intend
to take some sort of...

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.604
radical action
to protect the ocean.

00:37:55.673 --> 00:37:56.935
Then I presume

00:37:57.041 --> 00:37:59.976
you intend to take
radical action to stop them.

00:38:17.695 --> 00:38:21.222
Warning. Hull pressure
approaching critical.

00:38:21.299 --> 00:38:22.891
We're going down too fast.

00:38:23.034 --> 00:38:25.798
It's the only way we
can outrun your ships.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:29.431
Janeway to Paris.
Return to Voyager immediately.

00:38:29.507 --> 00:38:31.441
I'm sorry, Captain.

00:38:31.509 --> 00:38:33.136
I can't do that.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:37.146
Lieutenant, you are disobeying
a direct order.

00:38:37.214 --> 00:38:39.011
I know.

00:38:40.918 --> 00:38:41.885
He cut us off.

00:38:41.952 --> 00:38:43.249
What the hell are they up to?

00:38:43.321 --> 00:38:44.447
Looks like they're heading

00:38:44.522 --> 00:38:46.319
directly beneath
the industrial complex.

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:47.914
Can we reach them with phasers?

00:38:47.992 --> 00:38:49.118
Unadvisable.

00:38:49.193 --> 00:38:51.252
It would create
a hydro-dynamic shock wave.

00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:53.297
What about an old-fashioned
depth charge?

00:38:53.364 --> 00:38:55.832
It should be possible
to modify a photon torpedo.

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:57.424
We could program it to detonate

00:38:57.501 --> 00:38:59.594
once it's in proximity
to the Delta Flyer.

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:01.160
Do it.

00:39:07.211 --> 00:39:08.769
The torpedo is ready, Captain.

00:39:08.846 --> 00:39:11.679
However,
the Delta Flyer has submerged

00:39:11.749 --> 00:39:13.341
below our targeting range.

00:39:13.417 --> 00:39:15.214
Consul Burkus, hailing again.

00:39:15.286 --> 00:39:16.275
On screen.

00:39:16.354 --> 00:39:17.685
Our refinery workers

00:39:17.755 --> 00:39:20.883
have been given five minutes
to clear the structure.

00:39:21.025 --> 00:39:24.461
Was this the kind of evacuation
you had in mind, Captain?

00:39:24.528 --> 00:39:25.893
Can you get them out in time?

00:39:25.963 --> 00:39:27.157
Yes, but...

00:39:27.231 --> 00:39:28.323
Do it.

00:39:28.399 --> 00:39:29.661
I'll find a way
to protect your refinery.

00:39:29.734 --> 00:39:30.666
End transmission.

00:39:30.735 --> 00:39:31.429
Captain!

00:39:32.203 --> 00:39:33.534
He descended to avoid attack.

00:39:33.604 --> 00:39:35.003
If my calculations are correct,

00:39:35.072 --> 00:39:37.734
he will have to come back up
to a depth of 2,000 meters

00:39:37.808 --> 00:39:38.797
to strike his target.

00:39:38.876 --> 00:39:40.503
Giving us a window
of opportunity.

00:39:40.578 --> 00:39:41.636
Precisely.

00:39:41.712 --> 00:39:43.907
Captain, this is Tom
we're talking about.

00:39:44.048 --> 00:39:46.073
We're not going
to open fire, are we?

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:48.675
As far as I'm concerned,
he forfeited his status

00:39:48.753 --> 00:39:50.516
as a protected member
of this crew

00:39:50.588 --> 00:39:52.522
the second he launched
that shuttle.

00:39:57.595 --> 00:40:00.086
How long till we reach
our target?

00:40:00.164 --> 00:40:02.428
36 seconds.

00:40:03.501 --> 00:40:05.526
He's started his ascent.

00:40:05.603 --> 00:40:06.695
Hail him.

00:40:06.771 --> 00:40:07.931
Go ahead.

00:40:08.005 --> 00:40:11.907
Lieutenant Paris,
this is your final warning.

00:40:11.976 --> 00:40:14.103
He's not responding.

00:40:14.178 --> 00:40:16.544
Arm the torpedo.

00:40:16.614 --> 00:40:19.515
20 seconds to weapons range.

00:40:19.583 --> 00:40:21.881
Are you sure you want
to go through with this?

00:40:23.421 --> 00:40:24.445
You?

00:40:24.522 --> 00:40:27.958
I've taken you this far.

00:40:28.025 --> 00:40:30.550
We better arm that missile.

00:40:35.433 --> 00:40:36.798
Missile armed.

00:40:36.867 --> 00:40:41.236
Stand down, Mr. Paris,
or I will open fire.

00:40:44.108 --> 00:40:47.407
Still no answer.

00:40:47.478 --> 00:40:49.309
Time.

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:51.644
Ten seconds.

00:40:51.715 --> 00:40:53.239
Nine...

00:40:53.317 --> 00:40:55.410
Eight... seven...

00:40:55.486 --> 00:40:56.976
six...

00:40:57.121 --> 00:40:58.247
Five...

00:40:58.322 --> 00:41:00.654
four... three...

00:41:00.724 --> 00:41:02.919
Two... one.

00:41:02.993 --> 00:41:03.925
Fire.

00:41:04.028 --> 00:41:04.722
Fire!

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:19.675
The Flyer has been disabled.

00:41:19.743 --> 00:41:21.176
Their missile?

00:41:21.245 --> 00:41:22.712
Deflected.

00:41:29.253 --> 00:41:33.952
Captain's Log, Stardate 52179.4.

00:41:34.024 --> 00:41:36.117
After salvaging the Delta Flyer,

00:41:36.193 --> 00:41:38.923
we've resumed our course
toward the Alpha Quadrant.

00:41:38.996 --> 00:41:42.090
I now have to turn my attention
to a matter of discipline.

00:41:43.868 --> 00:41:46.632
Lieutenant Thomas Eugene Paris,

00:41:46.704 --> 00:41:48.695
you are guilty
of insubordination,

00:41:48.772 --> 00:41:50.967
unauthorized use
of a spacecraft,

00:41:51.041 --> 00:41:52.201
reckless endangerment

00:41:52.276 --> 00:41:54.210
and conduct
unbecoming an officer.

00:41:54.278 --> 00:41:56.473
Do you have anything to say?

00:41:56.547 --> 00:41:57.741
Riga needed my help.

00:41:57.815 --> 00:41:59.976
In doing so, you disobeyed
my direct orders.

00:42:00.050 --> 00:42:01.244
Yes, ma'am.

00:42:01.318 --> 00:42:03.445
You violated the protocols
that govern this crew.

00:42:03.521 --> 00:42:04.783
Yes, ma'am.

00:42:04.855 --> 00:42:06.914
You nearly caused an armed
conflict with the Moneans.

00:42:07.024 --> 00:42:09.254
And frankly, you're lucky to
be standing here right now.

00:42:09.326 --> 00:42:11.294
I would have destroyed
your shuttle if necessary.

00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:15.162
Yes, ma'am.

00:42:15.232 --> 00:42:17.223
Permission to speak freely.

00:42:21.071 --> 00:42:23.539
Riga's people
weren't going to listen.

00:42:23.607 --> 00:42:24.904
They were going
to ignore our warnings.

00:42:24.975 --> 00:42:26.499
You don't know that.

00:42:26.577 --> 00:42:29.512
Riga knew, and I was the only
one who could help them.

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:30.706
I understand your passion,

00:42:30.781 --> 00:42:32.715
but passion alone
doesn't give you the right

00:42:32.783 --> 00:42:34.375
to take matters
into your own hands.

00:42:34.451 --> 00:42:36.578
Four years ago,
I released you from prison

00:42:36.654 --> 00:42:38.087
and gave you a fresh start.

00:42:38.155 --> 00:42:39.952
Until now, you've been
a fine officer.

00:42:40.057 --> 00:42:42.218
Your service on this ship
has been exemplary.

00:42:42.293 --> 00:42:44.921
I really believed you were
past this kind of conduct.

00:42:44.995 --> 00:42:48.590
I've never been very good
at playing by the rules.

00:42:48.666 --> 00:42:52.193
That doesn't mean
that serving under your command

00:42:52.269 --> 00:42:54.760
hasn't changed me...
for the better.

00:42:54.838 --> 00:42:58.239
At least this time,
I broke the rules for a reason,

00:42:58.309 --> 00:43:00.777
for something I believed in.

00:43:00.844 --> 00:43:02.812
I admire your principles, Tom,

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:05.348
but I can't ignore
what you've done.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:09.613
I hereby reduce you
to the rank of Ensign.

00:43:09.687 --> 00:43:13.282
And I sentence you
to 30 days solitary confinement.

00:43:13.357 --> 00:43:15.018
Take Ensign Paris to the brig.

00:43:17.394 --> 00:43:19.862
I know the way.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:24.791
Rise and shine, Ensign.

00:43:24.868 --> 00:43:26.893
Your 30 days have been served.

00:43:32.576 --> 00:43:34.840
You may want to shave first.

00:43:34.912 --> 00:43:36.209
Yes, sir.

00:43:48.859 --> 00:43:50.690
Torres to Paris.

00:43:50.761 --> 00:43:52.126
Go ahead.

00:43:52.196 --> 00:43:55.131
Rumor has it
that you're free for dinner.

00:43:55.199 --> 00:43:56.666
Gee, I don't know.

00:43:56.734 --> 00:44:00.465
Are you sure you want to be seen
associating with an ex-con?

00:44:00.537 --> 00:44:02.027
My quarters, 0700.

00:44:02.106 --> 00:44:04.131
That's an order, Ensign.

00:44:04.208 --> 00:44:06.335
Yes, ma'am.

00:44:13.717 --> 00:44:15.685
Resume recording.

00:44:19.723 --> 00:44:23.523
Well, I'm out now
and back to my duties.

00:44:28.732 --> 00:44:33.169
I honestly don't know
if I'll ever understand you

00:44:33.237 --> 00:44:37.173
or what went wrong between us,

00:44:37.241 --> 00:44:42.543
but I hope this letter helps you
understand me a little better.

00:44:50.754 --> 00:44:55.657
Computer, file letter
in my personal database...

00:44:57.561 --> 00:45:01.327
and transmit when we're
within range of Earth.
